{
  "dom_id": "step-q347239",
  "name": "Question 17",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195525/assets/61e7943162b7df06af1d6ba805630268e872031e1cb27b566810ee7fc22e6bbc.png)\n\nA piece of cardboard measures $18\\,ft\\times 18\\,ft.$ A tray of height $y\\,\\text{ft}$ is formed by cutting a $y\\times y\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}$ square from each of the corners and folding up the sides, as shown in the diagram. Determine the height $y$ of the box that gives the maximum possible volume.\n\n$y=$\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft}$",
  "worked_solution": "**Step 1**: We need to find the values of $x,y,$ and $z$ such that the volume\n\n$$\nV=xyz\n$$\n\nis the greatest possible value.\n\n**Step 2**: Notice that the width $x$ and the depth $z$ of the tray are given by\n\n$$\nx=18-2y,\\,z=18-2y.\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $x>0$ and $z>0,$ we must have that $18-2y>0$ and $18-2y>0$, hence $y<9.$ Therefore, the range of validity for $y$ is $0<y<9.$\n\n**Step 3**: Substituting the above into the expression for $V,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =xyz \\\\  & =(18-2y)(y)(18-2y) \\\\  & =4{y}^{3}-72{y}^{2}+324y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: We solve the equation ${V}^{′}(y)=0.$ Taking the first derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{V}^{′}(y)=12{y}^{2}-144y+324,\n$$\n\nand solving for the stationary points, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}^{′}(y) & =0 \\\\ 12{y}^{2}-144y+324 & =0 \\\\ {y}^{2}-12y+27 & =0 \\\\ y & =3,\\,9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe discard $9$ since we already know that $0<y<9.$\n\n**Step 5**: We confirm that $3$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\n{V}^{″}(y)=24y-144.\n$$\n\nSince ${V}^{″}(3)$ is negative, we conclude that $3$ is indeed a maximum of $V(y).$\n\nTherefore, the height of the box with the maximum volume is $y=3\\text{ ft}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
